# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Node
node_modules/
.next/
out/
.turbo/

# Secrets — NEVER commit (Section 13.3, D-019 secrets flow)
#
# Directory patterns here are ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose. An unanchored
# `secrets/` or `data/` matches at ANY depth, which silently excluded the source packages
# `backend/src/nexus/secrets/` and `backend/src/nexus/data/` from the repository: six modules
# the code imports were missing from a pushed commit, so a fresh clone could not import the
# package. The test suite did not catch it because it ran against the working tree, not the
# repository. `backend/tests/test_repo_hygiene.py` now guards this.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
/secrets/
/sops/
/age-keys/
*.age

# Data / artifacts (kept out of git; 200 GB free-tier cap, Feasibility §10.6 Z-04)
# Anchored for the same reason as above. Runtime data lives at the repo root or under
# backend/data, never inside backend/src.
/data/
/backend/data/
*.duckdb
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.parquet
*.zst
/backups/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- source tree is never an
# artifact. Last match wins in .gitignore, so these negations sit at the end and guarantee no
# pattern above can ever swallow a module again. (`__pycache__/` directories stay excluded:
# git never descends into an excluded directory, so files inside cannot be re-included.)
!backend/src/
!backend/src/**
!backend/migrations/
!backend/migrations/**

# ...but compiled artifacts inside the source tree stay out. These MUST come after the
# negations above: the last matching pattern wins, and a negation that re-includes a
# `__pycache__/` directory lets git descend into it and re-include every `.pyc` inside.
backend/src/**/__pycache__/
backend/src/**/*.py[cod]
backend/src/**/*.egg-info/
backend/migrations/**/__pycache__/
